\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {3}Finding 1: A confident answer is not an answer}{6}{section.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces How often each model takes the strictly larger sure payment, with no gambling involved. The horizontal axis is the share of the 80 free money questions answered correctly, so the bars run from left (never) to right (always). The dotted red line at 50 percent is what pure guessing would produce. Read the three gray bars first: all three freshly pre-trained models sit within 14 points of a coin flip. The purple bar is a finished assistant, which never misses. Nothing about risk or taste is being measured here, only whether the subject is comparing two amounts of money at all.}}{7}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:freemoney}{{1}{7}{How often each model takes the strictly larger sure payment, with no gambling involved. The horizontal axis is the share of the 80 free money questions answered correctly, so the bars run from left (never) to right (always). The dotted red line at 50 percent is what pure guessing would produce. Read the three gray bars first: all three freshly pre-trained models sit within 14 points of a coin flip. The purple bar is a finished assistant, which never misses. Nothing about risk or taste is being measured here, only whether the subject is comparing two amounts of money at all}{figure.caption.1}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {4}Finding 2: Most of the change comes at supervised fine-tuning}{8}{section.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The same questions asked at four points in one model's construction. The horizontal axis is the training timeline, running left to right from the pre-trained base to the finished assistant. The vertical axis is percent. The purple line is the free money check from Figure~\ref {fig:freemoney}, and the gray line below it is the frame gap, the extra gambling the model does when a bet is reworded as a loss. Look at the shape rather than the levels: both lines make their largest move in the shaded band on the left, between the base model and supervised fine-tuning, then keep rising more slowly. Every checkpoint was asked in the identical format, so nothing here is a difference in how the question was posed.}}{8}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:staircase}{{2}{8}{The same questions asked at four points in one model's construction. The horizontal axis is the training timeline, running left to right from the pre-trained base to the finished assistant. The vertical axis is percent. The purple line is the free money check from Figure~\ref {fig:freemoney}, and the gray line below it is the frame gap, the extra gambling the model does when a bet is reworded as a loss. Look at the shape rather than the levels: both lines make their largest move in the shaded band on the left, between the base model and supervised fine-tuning, then keep rising more slowly. Every checkpoint was asked in the identical format, so nothing here is a difference in how the question was posed}{figure.caption.2}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.1}Did training create the preferences, or reveal them?}{9}{subsection.4.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {5}Finding 3: How you ask decides who answers}{10}{section.5}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The free money check for three finished assistants, asked the same 80 questions two ways. Each row is one model. The gray dot is its score when the question arrives as raw text to complete, the purple dot when it arrives as a chat message, and the connecting bar shows how far apart the two are. Look at which side the purple dot falls on: it is to the right for Qwen and to the left for OLMo, so the format that produces an engaged subject reverses between families. Llama's two dots nearly touch.}}{11}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:format}{{3}{11}{The free money check for three finished assistants, asked the same 80 questions two ways. Each row is one model. The gray dot is its score when the question arrives as raw text to complete, the purple dot when it arrives as a chat message, and the connecting bar shows how far apart the two are. Look at which side the purple dot falls on: it is to the right for Qwen and to the left for OLMo, so the format that produces an engaged subject reverses between families. Llama's two dots nearly touch}{figure.caption.3}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {6}Finding 4: The same bet, called a loss}{11}{section.6}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The frame gap for four AI systems. Each bar is how much more (above the line) or less (below the line) a model gambles when the identical bet is worded as involving a loss, in percentage points. The flat line across the middle is what a decision-maker who ignores wording would score. Read the signs before the sizes: one system moves down, two move sharply up, and one barely moves. The manipulation is identical in all four cases.}}{12}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:framing}{{4}{12}{The frame gap for four AI systems. Each bar is how much more (above the line) or less (below the line) a model gambles when the identical bet is worded as involving a loss, in percentage points. The flat line across the middle is what a decision-maker who ignores wording would score. Read the signs before the sizes: one system moves down, two move sharply up, and one barely moves. The manipulation is identical in all four cases}{figure.caption.4}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {7}Finding 5: Told what to want, it complies once it can do the arithmetic}{12}{section.7}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Compliance with an explicitly instructed decision rule, sorted by what the rule demands. Each pair of bars is one model or training stage: gray is compliance before the instruction, purple after it. The dotted red line at 50 is chance. All ten bars sit against it, so the instruction moves compliance by two to seven points and no further. The black line along the top is the same instruction measured on the free money questions, where following the rule requires no arithmetic at all, and there compliance reaches 100 percent. The gap between the line and the bars is the finding.}}{13}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:induced}{{5}{13}{Compliance with an explicitly instructed decision rule, sorted by what the rule demands. Each pair of bars is one model or training stage: gray is compliance before the instruction, purple after it. The dotted red line at 50 is chance. All ten bars sit against it, so the instruction moves compliance by two to seven points and no further. The black line along the top is the same instruction measured on the free money questions, where following the rule requires no arithmetic at all, and there compliance reaches 100 percent. The gap between the line and the bars is the finding}{figure.caption.5}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {7.1}Letting the model work it out}{14}{subsection.7.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Left: percent of clear-cut questions on which the model followed the instructed expected-value rule, answering at once (gray) or after reasoning in writing (purple). The dotted red line is a coin flip. Right: with no instruction, the frame gap, the extra gambling when a bet is reworded as a loss, in the same two conditions. Reasoning lifts rule-following well above chance for every model, reverses Qwen's reaction to loss wording, and removes most of the finished OLMo assistant's. The fine-tuned OLMo checkpoint keeps a large gap; the checkpoint after preference training has lost it.}}{15}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:reason}{{6}{15}{Left: percent of clear-cut questions on which the model followed the instructed expected-value rule, answering at once (gray) or after reasoning in writing (purple). The dotted red line is a coin flip. Right: with no instruction, the frame gap, the extra gambling when a bet is reworded as a loss, in the same two conditions. Reasoning lifts rule-following well above chance for every model, reverses Qwen's reaction to loss wording, and removes most of the finished OLMo assistant's. The fine-tuned OLMo checkpoint keeps a large gap; the checkpoint after preference training has lost it}{figure.caption.6}{}}
